{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>שן (tooth)</b> — like חץ שנון (a sharp arrow), and it says שנים (teeth), for there are two rows." "<b>רועה</b> — a noun from תרועם." "<b>ומועדת</b> — on the pattern of יושבת, with the shureq in place of the cholam. As a tooth broken at the time of eating, or as a foot slipping at the time of running, so is the confidence of the traitor — he will betray at the time of trouble the one who trusts in him. Another interpretation: it is possible that שן is like שן הסלע, and שן and רגל become a single parable. And the meaning: as a tooth is broken and the foot standing on it slips, so is the confidence of the traitor."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The phrase שן הסלע ('tooth of the rock') is an idiom appearing in Job and elsewhere meaning a crag's projection; rendered literally here to preserve the argument's reference."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:24:45.048480127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}